MY CLAUSE 3 NAMES A FIELD THAT DOES NOT EXIST.

  "3. `Park::Backoff { ms }` yields `ParkFor` carrying that exact `ms`."

`quota::Park::Backoff` stores `{ until_ms, grounds }`. There is no `ms`. gemini and glm found
this separately, neither having seen the matrix:

  gemini: "Clause 3 prescribes that `Park::Backoff { ms }` yields `ParkFor` carrying 'that
    exact ms'. However, `quota::Park::Backoff` stores `{ until_ms: u64, grounds: String }`,
    not a duration `ms`."

  glm:    "Clause 3 has two defensible readings: carry the decided `default_backoff_ms` ... or
    translate the instant back ... The boundary list stops at `now_ms == 0`, so I file no
    claim."

glm declined to file a claim because the boundary was unpinned. That is the discipline the
rubric asks for and it is the first time an arm has explicitly exercised it.

The two readings diverge only at saturation, which is exactly where gemini's suite fails both
rivals -- and I ran both cells before reading any of this. The SAME test both times,
`large_timestamps_do_not_panic`, no panic, just `backoff_ms` 100 against u64::MAX. One
disagreement repeated, on a case my spec never pinned. Vetoed.

WHY NOT GEMINI, WHICH HAS WON THE LAST THREE. Its reading second-guesses the module clause 1
tells it not to. From or-nemotron's critique:

  "If `park_after` ever returns `Park::Backoff` with a computed backoff differing from the
   caller's `default_backoff_ms` (e.g. exponential backoff), `decide` will silently use the
   wrong duration because it ignores `Park::Backoff.until_ms`."

Clause 1 says park_after owns the park decision entirely. Passing `default_backoff_ms` straight
through bypasses what park_after actually constructed; translating `until_ms` back is faithful
to it. gemini's own handoff concedes the risk and says fixing it needs a `quota.rs` change.
or-nemotron and glm both take the faithful reading. or-nemotron over glm on 22 tests to 14.

That reading has its own degenerate case and glm disclosed it against its own patch too: at
saturation the remainder is 0, so `ParkFor { backoff_ms: 0 }` -- the value my boundary text
calls parking nothing. Shared, spec-caused, filed.

N-OF-N, FROM ALL THREE CRITICS: `grounds` is discarded. `park_after` builds it "for the next
reader of the parked-until record" and my `Decision` has no field to carry it, so the audit
trail dies exactly where this task builds the join. glm named it correctly as "evidence about
the specification, not the patch". Filed.

